---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import uk from "../../../../data/targets/uk.json";
import us from "../../../../data/targets/us.json";
import eu from "../../../../data/targets/eu.json";
import meta from "../../../../data/meta.json";
import References from "../../components/References.astro";
import { cite } from "../../lib/references.ts";
const standards = [uk, us, eu];
---
<Base title="Methods and sources" current="about">
  <h1>Methods and sources</h1>
  <p class="lede">How the nutrient planner works, where its numbers come from, and what it can't tell you.</p>

  <h2>Food composition data</h2>
  <p>
    Nutrient values come from the US Department of Agriculture's FoodData Central (public domain), SR Legacy
    dataset <Fragment set:html={cite("usda-sr")} />. About 115 cheap, common, UK-available foods are hand-matched to USDA records, given
    friendly names and practical units, and switched on by default. Around 2,100 more vegetables, fruits, pulses,
    grains, breads, nuts, seeds, meat, fish, dairy foods, eggs, oils, herbs and spices are added automatically by
    rules that keep cooked lean cuts of meat and leave out baby foods, branded and heavily processed products;
    these are off by default and can be switched on in the planner's food list. Choosing vegetarian hides meat and
    fish; choosing vegan also hides dairy, eggs and honey. The food list, the rules and a match report live in the repository's
    <code>data/</code> folder so anyone can check them.
  </p>
  <p class="muted small">
    Current snapshot: {meta.foodsLastUpdated ? `built ${meta.foodsLastUpdated}, ${meta.foodCount} foods` : "not built yet"}.
    The snapshot is stored in the repository so the site keeps working even if the USDA servers change, and is refreshed every three months.
  </p>

  <h2>Recommended intakes</h2>
  {standards.map((s) => (
    <section>
      <h3>{s.longName}</h3>
      <p class="small">
        {s.verified?.checked
          ? <><strong>Checked</strong> line by line against the original document ({s.verified.against}) on {s.verified.date}.</>
          : <><strong>Not yet checked</strong> against the original document. {s.verified?.against}.</>}
        {s.verified?.pending && <> <strong>Not yet checked:</strong> {s.verified.pending}</>}
      </p>
      <ul class="prose">
        {s.sources.map((src) => <li>{src.role ? `${src.role}: ` : ""}{src.title} <Fragment set:html={cite(src.id)} /></li>)}
      </ul>
      <ul class="prose muted small">{s.notes.map((n) => <li>{n}</li>)}</ul>
    </section>
  ))}
  <p>
    The charts can also show the other two standards' targets for the same person (same age, sex, weight and
    calories) as thin coloured outlines, so you can see where the UK, US and EU agree and where they differ.
    The planner always optimises against the one standard you choose.
  </p>

  <h2>How the planner chooses foods</h2>
  <p>
    The planner solves a mixed-integer programme with the HiGHS solver <Fragment set:html={cite("highs")} />, running in your browser.
    It chooses at most your chosen number of ingredients and an amount of each, in practical steps
    (10 g, half an egg, a slice of bread), to minimise the total <em>relative</em> shortfall across all
    minimum targets, so being 20% short of iron counts the same as being 20% short of protein. Upper limits
    (salt, saturated fat, tolerable upper intake levels) and your calorie range are never exceeded.
    Further options are found by forbidding most of the ingredients of earlier options.
  </p>
  <h2>Reading the charts</h2>
  <p>
    Each radar chart shows every nutrient as a percentage of its target. The thick navy ring is the target
    (100%). The scale is linear and runs to 150% unless a value is higher, in which case it grows to fit, so
    400% always looks larger than 300%; "Zoom to target" shows the area around 100% instead and marks anything
    beyond the edge with an arrow. Green points meet their goal and red ones don't. "≥" means aim for at least
    the target; "≤" means stay at or below it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The "At a glance" chart follows the nutrition declaration on UK and EU food labels (Regulation 1169/2011) <Fragment set:html={cite("eu-1169")} />:
    energy, fat, saturates, carbohydrate, sugars, fibre, protein and salt. Each is compared with the official
    recommendation for the person where one exists; total sugars, which has no recommendation, is compared with
    the label's adult reference intake (90 g) and shown in grey. Vitamins, minerals and essential amino acids
    (the building blocks of protein) each have their own chart below it, with a table showing which foods
    provide each nutrient.
  </p>

  <h2>Limitations</h2>
  <p>The results are estimates. Please treat them as a starting point for thinking about your food, not as a precise measurement.</p>
  <ul class="prose">
    <li><strong>Borrowed safety limits.</strong> The UK and EU tables used here don't give a complete set of upper limits, so the US tolerable upper intake levels <Fragment set:html={cite("nasem-dri")} /> are used for safety checks in every standard.</li>
    <li><strong>US food data, UK shops.</strong> Fortification differs between countries (for example US milk usually has added vitamin D; UK milk doesn't), and brands differ. Fortified products should be checked against their labels.</li>
    <li><strong>Iodine and some other nutrients</strong> are missing from most USDA records, so they are listed as "not checked" rather than guessed. Where a food has no value for a nutrient, it counts as zero, which can understate the total.</li>
    <li><strong>Absorption isn't modelled yet.</strong> Iron from plants is absorbed less well than iron from meat, ALA omega-3 converts poorly to EPA and DHA, and phytates reduce zinc absorption. These are planned refinements.</li>
    <li><strong>Amino acid targets</strong> use WHO adult requirements per kg of body weight <Fragment set:html={cite("who-2007")} /> and are applied to adults only. They haven't yet been checked against the original WHO report.</li>
    <li><strong>Reference values are for healthy populations.</strong> If you have a medical condition, are pregnant, or are planning food for a child, check with a doctor, midwife or registered dietitian.</li>
  </ul>

  <References />
</Base>
